# HyperTransfer

HyperTransfer copies a file only while a RDMA port is ACTIVE, then compares three measurements of the data fork: the source before the copy, the received file, and the source again. Each measurement is two reads that must return the same bytes, digested with SHA-512 and BLAKE2b.

A one-sided WRITE is not finished when the sender's completion arrives. Publish a 64-byte completion record after a flush read of the tail. The receiver writes the partial file only after that record is visible and the CPU-visible bytes match it. Rename the partial onto the finished name only when the three digests agree.

Thunderbolt Internet Protocol is a link. It is not a reason to open the file panes or to send the file. File bytes stay off sockets. UDP 24764 on the Thunderbolt bridge carries the 64-byte queue-pair record and then closes.

```bash
swift run --disable-sandbox HyperTransferChecks
```

This runs on the M4 Pro Mac mini with 24 GB unified memory. It does not load a model. Do not invent a port.

